2 - Factorize cli
3 - Extend the red flags :


-------------------- Done red flags

🧾 Balance Sheet Health

Debt > Market Cap → automatic red flag.

Debt/Equity > 1.0 → capital structure too leveraged (unless it's a financial).

Current Ratio < 1.0 → potential liquidity problems.

Quick Ratio < 0.8 → can’t meet short-term obligations without selling inventory.

Negative Tangible Book Value → high intangibles / goodwill masking weak core assets.

Interest Coverage (EBIT / Interest Expense) < 2x → risk of default.

Rising Total Liabilities faster than Total Assets → deterioration of financial position.

Significant off-balance-sheet liabilities (leases, pensions, etc.) → check footnotes (SEC filings).

💸 Cash Flow Red Flags

Operating Cash Flow < Net Income consistently → earnings quality issues.

Free Cash Flow (FCF) negative for 2+ years → burning cash.

Dividends > Free Cash Flow → unsustainable payout.

CapEx rising faster than operating cash flow → heavy reinvestment with poor returns.

Stock-based compensation > 10% of operating cash flow → dilution disguised as expense.

Frequent share issuance → management funding growth via dilution, not profits.

📈 Income Statement / Profitability

Declining gross margin → pricing pressure or cost inflation.

Declining operating margin → poor cost control or competition.

Net margin < 3% (except utilities, grocers, etc.) → too thin to handle downturns.

ROE < 10% and ROIC < 7% → poor capital efficiency.

One-time gains/losses repeatedly appearing → earnings manipulation.

Extraordinary items > 10% of income → low-quality earnings.

Revenue growth without EPS growth → margin compression or share dilution.

Gross margin or EPS highly volatile year-to-year → unstable business.



-------------------- TO ADD (whatever is not NLAP dependent yet)


🧠 Management / Governance

High or rising SG&A % of revenue → bloated overhead or poor discipline.

Executive compensation rising faster than net income → shareholder-unfriendly.

Insiders selling heavily / low insider ownership → lack of confidence.

Auditor changes / restatements → accounting red flags.

Frequent acquisitions without accretion → empire building, not value creation.

Share buybacks at high valuations / issuing shares at low valuations → poor capital allocation.

No clear or improving ROIC trend post-acquisition → poor integration.

🧮 Valuation Traps

PE ratio < 5 with no growth catalyst → might be a value trap.

PEG ratio > 2.0 → overpriced relative to growth.

Price/Book > 5 without high ROE or moat → speculative pricing.

Dividend yield > 8% → market pricing in dividend cut.

Enterprise Value / EBITDA > 15x → expensive, unless hyper-growth.

Price/Sales > 10 → needs massive growth to justify.

🧯 Growth & Sustainability

Revenue CAGR < inflation over 5 years → stagnation.

Earnings or revenue growth dependent on one-time items → unsustainable.

Customer or product concentration (>30%) → fragile business.

High cyclicality without strong balance sheet → danger in downturns.

Declining R&D or capex while claiming growth → underinvesting.

🌎 Qualitative / Contextual

Business model difficult to understand (Buffett rule).

No competitive advantage / moat erosion → low pricing power.

Dependent on commodity prices → little control over margins.

High regulatory risk / litigation exposure → unpredictable.

Inconsistent communication or misleading investor relations → integrity risk.

High short interest (>10%) → market skepticism.

Sudden executive departures → internal trouble.

⚠️ Absolute Dealbreakers

Persistent negative free cash flow + rising debt.

Accounting irregularities / restatements.

Dividends paid from debt or equity issuance.

Management hiding details or using excessive “adjusted” metrics.

Material weakness in internal controls (SEC 10-K Item 9A).